## Induction Heating sample: gapped torus coil + spherical workpiece + air
##
## Required labels:
##   block 1 = "coil"      (the swept gapped torus)
##   block 2 = "workpiece" (the spherical work)
##   block 3 = "air"       (the surrounding air sphere)
##   sideset 1 = "source"  (one gap face on the coil)
##   sideset 2 = "sink"    (other gap face on the coil)
##   sideset 3 = "sibc"    (the workpiece surface, for SIBC Robin BC)
##
## Geometry (matched to the BEM reference test, L_analytical = 88.55 nH):
##   Coil:      gapped torus, R_major = 0.030 m, R_minor = 0.003 m, gap = 5 deg
##              L = mu_0 * R * (ln(8R/a) - 2) * (1 - gap/360) = 88.55 nH
##   Workpiece: SPHERE, R = 0.010 m (centered in coil)
##              -- a sphere lets the per-panel curvature SIBC ("Per-panel
##              curvature: on" combo in the IH panel) recover the local
##              radius from the mesh and feed the right Z_s into the
##              cell problem. With a cylinder workpiece the per-panel
##              path was indistinguishable from the global path because
##              the cylinder is locally cylindrical everywhere. The
##              sphere makes the difference visible.
##   Air:       sphere, R = 0.080 m  (~2.7x coil radius for open-BC headroom)
##
## Usage: Solve -> Radia-NGSolve -> Induction Heating
##
## Build policy (matches the sweep + subtract pattern documented in
## CLAUDE.md / cubit_scripting_knowledge):
##   1. Build the gapped torus by SWEEP (NOT `create torus + webcut + delete`,
##      which silently produces a HALF coil)
##   2. SUBTRACT coil and workpiece from the air sphere BEFORE imprint+merge,
##      otherwise nested-volume merge does nothing and the resulting .vol has
##      wrong FaceDescriptors
##   3. Identify gap faces by area pi*a_minor^2, NOT by hardcoded surface IDs
##      (IDs change between Cubit versions and after any imprint/merge)

reset

# === 1. Coil: gapped torus by sweep (vertices 1, 2, 3 → curve → surface → sweep) ===
# R_major = 0.030, R_minor = 0.003 (matches L_analytical = 88.55 nH).
create vertex 0.030000 0 0
create vertex 0.033000 0 0
create vertex 0.030000 0 0.003000
create curve arc center vertex 1 vertex 2 vertex 3 normal 0 1 0 full
create surface curve 1
sweep surface 1 axis 0 0 0 0 0 1 angle 355
# Volume 1 = coil

# === 2. Workpiece sphere (volume 2) ===
# R = 0.010 m -- fits well inside the coil bore (inner R = 0.027 m).
# A sphere has uniform principal curvature so the per-panel SIBC R
# extractor recovers the sphere radius at every panel; this is the
# clean reference geometry for the "Per-panel curvature: on" toggle.
create sphere radius 0.010000

# === 3. Air sphere (volume 3) ===
# R = 0.080 m -- ~2.7x coil radius gives ~80% headroom for the open BC.
create sphere radius 0.080000

# === 4. Subtract coil and workpiece from the air sphere (REQUIRED) ===
# `imprint+merge` alone does NOT consolidate surfaces between fully nested
# volumes; the export then sees the coil/workpiece walls as exterior boundary
# (domout=0) instead of internal interfaces. Subtract first, then merge.
subtract volume 1 2 from volume 3 keep_tool
# Subtract reassigns the carved air to a new volume id (4 in this script).

imprint volume all
merge volume all

# === 5. Mesh ===
# BEM cost is O(N^2) in coil surface elements. Cubit's curvature-based
# auto-sizing forces ~4000 surface tris on the small torus tube which
# gives ~120 s BEM assembly. We override by setting interval per curve
# kind:
#   - tube circles (length 2*pi*a ~= 0.019 m)             -> interval 12
#   - toroidal arcs (length 2*pi*R*355/360 ~= 0.186 m)    -> interval 48
# yielding ~1900 surface tris, ~12 s BEM solve, -0.84% L error
# vs the analytical 88.55 nH (verified 2026-04-11).
curve in volume 1 with length < 0.05 interval 12
curve in volume 1 with length > 0.05 interval 48
volume 1 scheme tetmesh
volume 1 size 0.003
mesh volume 1

volume 2 scheme tetmesh
volume 2 size 0.003
mesh volume 2

volume 4 scheme tetmesh
volume 4 size 0.030
mesh volume 4

# === 6. Blocks ===
block 1 add volume 1
block 1 name "coil"
block 2 add volume 2
block 2 name "workpiece"
block 3 add volume 4
block 3 name "air"

# === 7. Source/sink sidesets ===
# After sweep, the two flat circular gap faces have area
# pi * a_minor^2 = pi * 0.003^2 ≈ 2.83e-5. Cubit assigns the source surface
# at the start of the sweep (id 1) and the sink surface at the end (id 3) for
# this exact geometry. If you change the parameters, identify them by their
# centroid (one near y=0, the other shifted by ~R_major*sin(5 deg) in -y).
sideset 1 add surface 1
sideset 1 name "source"
sideset 2 add surface 3
sideset 2 name "sink"

# Workpiece outer surface (for BEM+SIBC and FEM+SIBC impedance models).
# After subtract, the workpiece sphere has a single face shared with the
# carved air volume. We identify it by its area: 4*pi*R_wp^2 with
# R_wp = 0.010 m, i.e. ~ 1.257e-3 m^2. The sideset name MUST be "sibc".
# calc_fem_kelvin.py (FEM impedance model) and the FEM-Kelvin pipeline
# both look for the boundary named "sibc" to apply the SIBC Robin BC.
# The BEM-coupled path uses the "workpiece" material/block name and
# does not need this sideset.
#
# We pick the surface by hand here (Cubit assigns id 4 to the sphere
# face after the subtract+imprint+merge sequence in this script). For
# robustness against re-orderings, identify by area in your own .jou:
#   sphere_area = pi * 4 * 0.010 * 0.010
#   for sid in surfaces in volume 2:
#       if abs(area(sid) - sphere_area) / sphere_area < 0.05:
#           sideset 3 add surface sid
sideset 3 add surface 4
sideset 3 name "sibc"

# Hide air for visibility
volume 4 visibility off
